Agents: "canon schema --json" describes every command; "canon skills show" prints the guide.

Move an already-committed finding's disposition (promoted in the same run under `write: { promote: auto }` or with --promote; under `write.promote: manual`, commit it with `canon gate promote`)

Usage: canon finding close [OPTIONS] --round <N> --seq <N> --disposition <DISPOSITION> <CHANGE_ID>

Arguments:
  <CHANGE_ID>  The change under review — first component of the natural key

Options:
      --as <ROLE>                  The role you act as: planner, implementer, reviewer or orchestrator. Overrides `CANON_ROLE`. `schema` and `context` list only the role's commands, `status` keeps its steps, and `--role` on `evidence add` / `evidence audit` / `review scenario` / `review change` is this role unless given [possible values: planner, implementer, reviewer, orchestrator]
      --round <N>                  Which review round raised it (1-based)
      --seq <N>                    The finding's index within the round (1-based)
      --disposition <DISPOSITION>  Where it lands: fixed / rejected / deferred / open (reopening is a real transition). fixed records --resolution-rev, default HEAD, and only fixed may carry one [possible values: open, fixed, rejected, deferred]
      --resolution-rev <REV>       The commit that closed it, any git revision (resolved to the full sha, with the code tree it saw). Permitted only with --disposition fixed, where it defaults to HEAD. Existence only; canon never reads the commit's message or diff
      --reason <TEXT>              Why it was closed, one line. Required for rejected and deferred, optional for fixed and open; stored on the closing version and shown by `finding list`
      --actor <ACTOR_ID>           The actor recorded as authoring the TRANSITION — not the original finding's author, which is preserved on the record this supersedes [default: canon]
      --role <ROLE>                Attribution only — no partition key derives from it [default: reviewer]
      --session <SESSION_ID>       The session that authored the TRANSITION (recorded as actor.session_id)
      --promote                    Promote the record this run staged, in the same run; records others staged stay staged (the default under canon.yaml `write: { promote: auto }`)
      --no-promote                 Keep this write staged even under canon.yaml `write: { promote: auto }`
      --repo <DIR>                 The repo to act on (default: the nearest ancestor directory holding a canon.yaml)
      --json                       Output JSON instead of the human-readable form
      --input <JSON|FILE|->        The arguments as JSON: an object (one run) or an array / NDJSON file or `-` (a batch); keys are the option names in camelCase, `operands` an array
  -h, --help                       Print help
  -V, --version                    Print version

A finding is raised `open` and later closed. This is the ONLY way to record
that second state: `canon finding add` refuses an occupied natural key,
because two DIFFERENT findings under one (change, round, seq) are two
reviewers' work collapsed into one identity.

What it changes: the disposition, `resolution_sha` with it, and the closing
--reason (required for rejected and deferred). NOTHING else.
Severity, reviewer, summary, --rev, --introduced-rev and --file-ref
are READ from the committed record and copied byte-identically — this command
cannot edit them, and no command can. A finding recorded wrongly stays
wrongly recorded; that is the append-only ledger working, not a gap.

The committed record STAYS. This appends a second version at the same
natural key, and the pair IS the history: `open` at one instant, `fixed` at
another, each with its own author and timestamp. Every reader folds by
natural key to the latest version, so the finding is still counted once.

`canon gate promote` re-derives the transition rule itself before committing
the second version, so a hand-written body must clear the same bar.

Examples:
  canon finding close s44-spec-derived-worklist --round 1 --seq 1 \
      --disposition fixed --resolution-rev $(git rev-parse HEAD)
  canon finding close s44-spec-derived-worklist --round 1 --seq 9 \
      --disposition rejected --reason "not a defect"
